#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087387 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21741" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 2$ and $Q(x) = 2e^{-2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = e^{2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}\\cdot y & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087388 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89446fd7-75c2-5da5-bd97-469100f3ca7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21742" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3}) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x,$ and $v = \\sin x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087389 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09d09b0b-2132-59b2-9eb9-ae0748f09252" :question/math-academy-id "e-21743" :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 3x^2y = 9x^2, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 3x^2$ and $Q(x) = 9x^2.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x) = e^{x^3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}y) & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 9{x}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot y & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}}) & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x,$ making use of the substitution $u = x^3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =\\int 3{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{u}+C \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{{x}^{3}}+C \\\\ y & =3+C{e}^{-{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0) = 1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3+C{e}^{-{0}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 3 - 2e^{-x^3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c729da1-b189-5a4e-94d2-64eb262f3e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\cdot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319472} {:answer/id #uuid "714003c3-4547-5715-867d-df3ec93fb469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2x)=2x\\cdot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319473} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d73794-ac5f-5330-8698-3da3ed86be64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx)=x\\cdot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319474} {:answer/id #uuid "572b46c7-a5ff-511e-86ef-a90fedc8e4f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2)=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319475} {:answer/id #uuid "44692f8f-a4b4-57f5-8e53-8d4f1430bfed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{2x})={e}^{2x}\\cdot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c729da1-b189-5a4e-94d2-64eb262f3e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\cdot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319472} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddcf970f-163a-5613-ba77-753afbf5f553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ed12697-47e6-57ab-8aa1-251a21737d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-17533" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\nx\\frac{dy}{dx}+xy={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+y=x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=x.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 1\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}+y) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot x \\\\ {e}^{x}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{e}^{x}y & ={e}^{x}\\cdot x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27193621-7db1-59b0-9c37-bc01754a95a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319479} {:answer/id #uuid "73486bfb-1bc2-5bb9-8f92-19ef4ccbbbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319480} {:answer/id #uuid "78fae640-fd02-5b2f-a461-012893fe67f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319481} {:answer/id #uuid "f515e366-cb44-542e-a86f-d69f33dff88d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x})={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319482} {:answer/id #uuid "08464b2e-5100-5517-860d-5ef8fc386118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73486bfb-1bc2-5bb9-8f92-19ef4ccbbbc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319480} :answer-field/id #uuid "559083b4-0517-552d-a76e-d2a32c8a614a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c114cdd-b7ed-5540-974b-ac61cb7f42d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-17539" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+xy={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)=x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y) & =x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(yx) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01925a7d-a612-52a2-b163-7ba4b20905e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}))=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319486} {:answer/id #uuid "ad657640-a582-5d2d-8c2c-930433ee7555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x/2})={e}^{x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319487} {:answer/id #uuid "e69de45f-382f-5762-af8d-637a57ac8dff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2}))=-\\frac{x}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319488} {:answer/id #uuid "8be2ed15-9600-5839-968c-3a840d4e9af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2)=2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319489} {:answer/id #uuid "2946ba24-6fca-5bfd-82d5-2e45c90c564e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2})={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2946ba24-6fca-5bfd-82d5-2e45c90c564e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2})={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319490} :answer-field/id #uuid "30071098-17b9-5305-9211-94435ca3061c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3459142-ca31-57c5-a3d6-301c55017bc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-335278" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{2}y={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{3}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1/2)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{2}y) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{-x/2}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot y & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2}) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "575a9363-93be-50bd-b025-4633f010e32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319493} {:answer/id #uuid "88ccfeab-be0b-57f8-8cec-8e46e40fde65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319494} {:answer/id #uuid "c4029cb4-fe16-5f5d-8466-368361a3da8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319495} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e14a8e-c6da-5594-94a3-31a87c06e12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4029cb4-fe16-5f5d-8466-368361a3da8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319495} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d33421d-5645-527f-891d-cbce24c7df25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319492} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37448051-b6a0-516c-a51f-262f1a7b543d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{-x}+C{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319498} {:answer/id #uuid "b62b56ee-136a-56d9-9cbb-9b14378ef6d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{2x}+C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319499} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e4d9f6-eb82-5a10-9f7a-fb31e13a35e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{-2x}+C{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319500} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2bf5c0-947b-55af-987b-e8742d17f58e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{2x}+C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c2bf5c0-947b-55af-987b-e8742d17f58e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{e}^{2x}+C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319501} :answer-field/id #uuid "d577595a-12bd-50fb-96b0-7e6791e9d0d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6530e5db-b521-5f5c-99f7-59872d852e59" :question/math-academy-id "q-335402" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=6{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is already in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-3$ and $Q(x)=6{e}^{2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{-3x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y) & ={e}^{-3x}\\cdot 6{e}^{2x} \\\\ {e}^{-3x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{e}^{-3x}\\cdot y & =6{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-3x}) & =6{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{-3x} & =-6{e}^{-x}+C \\\\ y & =-6{e}^{2x}+C{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "322307e0-2dd4-556e-87a7-6bbb0563cf06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319504} {:answer/id #uuid "705695b6-e392-5c6a-b5d5-2e96e9cc7044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319505} {:answer/id #uuid "166e637d-b668-55e6-989f-23eb78e98ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2/x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319506} {:answer/id #uuid "27eea719-1a05-5ea7-abe2-69d814e82c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27eea719-1a05-5ea7-abe2-69d814e82c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319507} :answer-field/id #uuid "de5155f0-eb54-5135-87a6-d33277f1533f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319503} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f2cdcb9-9f83-5ef8-8ff4-bc6b0f4c6cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319509} {:answer/id #uuid "c70ee9e1-5d7b-5944-af98-a1ab861f49d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}+\\frac{C}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319510} {:answer/id #uuid "0a7c0901-d23c-5204-8677-ad8c8b86e62a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319511} {:answer/id #uuid "c254a346-9a01-5cb8-be81-cadbca5bedd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c254a346-9a01-5cb8-be81-cadbca5bedd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319512} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e733161-63b4-5fa8-a9af-bb5718b96480" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "68d33cb4-f198-5630-836f-f4155551348b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335409" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int {u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nLet $u=x,{u}^{′}=1,{v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and $v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2} & =\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & =x(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e60d8a77-b8ac-51ec-b3be-14d01efce466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319515} {:answer/id #uuid "d3038142-72da-511b-8701-976b54e116af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319516} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c6a6ae-2f50-528b-9fdc-0e62e28b9bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319517} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5aebf3-a934-57ce-a4b4-bd7fed1fed9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3/x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3038142-72da-511b-8701-976b54e116af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319516} :answer-field/id #uuid "a47dd53a-2288-5a53-a659-01940e193902" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319514} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edc8939a-a96e-5d4e-b14c-ad9a4ffebdd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}({e}^{x}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319520} {:answer/id #uuid "9873692a-25ba-5d98-8324-87d1eece533d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319521} {:answer/id #uuid "92073477-c596-5120-b0a4-9400a23c9489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}({e}^{x}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319522} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd17f32-0010-522e-90b6-a1d35e059592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9873692a-25ba-5d98-8324-87d1eece533d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319521} :answer-field/id #uuid "22df1977-64c8-51b4-88fc-99ab6d2ef37b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6a5ab99-8665-59c6-9abd-475eeed72999" :question/math-academy-id "q-335412" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{3})\\,dx & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & ={e}^{x}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98d3da2f-c3bc-55d0-b345-aeb8df1e3b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319526} {:answer/id #uuid "b51aef8b-7033-5481-96ec-98692a0f458b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319527} {:answer/id #uuid "29d11ed9-b43e-56b8-b040-a3bb95c2c6fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319528} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8d4b10-d0e9-5b3b-9cd3-8daf6602f605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98d3da2f-c3bc-55d0-b345-aeb8df1e3b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319526} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bcffd45-7313-58fe-b129-d00d842457aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319525} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275d1f70-bc23-53cd-ba1f-99addafe53b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319531} {:answer/id #uuid "5c22d022-4ede-517f-9415-efb2a7dffde1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-2){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319532} {:answer/id #uuid "6574f63e-001d-54ef-8adc-14776bffce0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319533} {:answer/id #uuid "c179d943-d3f7-5ac9-8925-1eeaa927ee2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319534} {:answer/id #uuid "7a627542-99dc-55e3-891b-bf7dfa7c002d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a627542-99dc-55e3-891b-bf7dfa7c002d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319535} :answer-field/id #uuid "460f78f1-4d35-5460-a331-1de288db6f4d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c6ef87c-1a0b-5973-8a1d-74f719874948" :question/math-academy-id "q-335806" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=2x{e}^{-x},\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=2x{e}^{-x}.$ \n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 2x{e}^{-x} \\\\ {e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{x}y & =2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x}) & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x})\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{x} & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ y & =({x}^{2}+C){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =({0}^{2}+C){e}^{-0} \\\\ -2 & =(0+C)\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=({x}^{2}-2){e}^{-x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85f19168-f0bb-53bf-9956-a7a50c6b83ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + (1)/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085450} {:answer/id #uuid "897d2937-d3bb-5f1d-849c-85706af227a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x - (1)/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085451} {:answer/id #uuid "de1ec696-f652-50e2-a2ca-f60ae1ce6225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + (1)/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085452} {:answer/id #uuid "f57e25a5-9568-5240-a324-3f22961eb106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{2} - (1)/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de1ec696-f652-50e2-a2ca-f60ae1ce6225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + (1)/(x^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085452} :answer-field/id #uuid "18274cfc-b655-5f0f-a270-2991bcbf13c5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085449} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9415bae7-50c8-54d0-83dc-c31d25edcec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085455} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecdf039-5776-5aeb-ba13-590c15e25f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085456} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d29939-8e0d-5470-8a46-7296d8eea87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085457} {:answer/id #uuid "d68c0ed3-905c-539d-a8c8-cb5c3575856b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6d29939-8e0d-5470-8a46-7296d8eea87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085457} :answer-field/id #uuid "9abd4e3a-2a51-563b-82f6-79ed2f082bba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bd80c89-2759-5cf8-8ba7-8fb4c49482e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335810" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\nx\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The first step is to write the equation in standard form,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,.\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y & =4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}(x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & =\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot 4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 4x \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & =4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & ={x}^{4}+C \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(1)=2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={1}^{2}+\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =1+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f49f20-cf6f-5c8f-960a-51ac5d6ce52b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319538} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b4bf7b-8751-5a29-8836-7198f3bedcf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319539} {:answer/id #uuid "9087d1e7-4ca8-58f1-ad60-9c68a35f35f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319540} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc95241-a36f-5601-a8dd-0bc86765db39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9087d1e7-4ca8-58f1-ad60-9c68a35f35f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319540} :answer-field/id #uuid "652299f1-a511-508c-80ec-e69295208912" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319537} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "638227f3-14b1-57ad-b134-5476d8e908f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319543} {:answer/id #uuid "17e12729-0837-5aad-99c2-355eb82e5937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319544} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b26751-74e4-5cd7-be5a-091f1ceb6b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319545} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5a2306-6662-526f-b179-e68ab24272ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17e12729-0837-5aad-99c2-355eb82e5937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319544} :answer-field/id #uuid "2477d20a-77cc-5754-932f-47114458bb76" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d18710c-01b9-5959-b4f5-a38740fd2b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-335811" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2y}{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}},\\,y(\\pi )=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}.$ \n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(\\pi )=0$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+C}{{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{C}{{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d8fd376-f47d-57e1-9b78-1afa0129b97d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319549} {:answer/id #uuid "83f68197-651d-55a3-a507-deabd92bdd10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319550} {:answer/id #uuid "db702fda-0d40-5b7e-b077-fb97944c0a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319551} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1151cd-61da-5475-807c-fe26159414af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db702fda-0d40-5b7e-b077-fb97944c0a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319551} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebcbec9c-fd96-5eca-a3b3-5246f9620bf2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319548} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "702a4745-95e1-5ff5-a1ff-cb59a8736923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319554} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a8bece-a0dc-5cb4-b7f7-779a2e18242d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319555} {:answer/id #uuid "a231487a-3f21-58e4-9c2c-a5c7f875dbf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319556} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d09860-63bf-5a67-b06d-93874d65db92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0d09860-63bf-5a67-b06d-93874d65db92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319557} :answer-field/id #uuid "15b34bf6-34d7-5179-8425-1fe9b7f4ba0b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2162d333-a174-5ff0-ac6f-16de19141bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-335812" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x},\\,x>0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y) & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ yx & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2C}{\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01c5809f-cb10-5192-b646-13036813137d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2747458-9353-553b-b791-2798fc1f2012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319560} {:answer/id #uuid "19613cb7-4c40-5228-9a48-8c72b0865012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319561} {:answer/id #uuid "8169f72e-d2fc-59f5-b86c-a67ec49a3ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319562} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b1b308-3d12-5a98-bdcb-880bf88794cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8169f72e-d2fc-59f5-b86c-a67ec49a3ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319562} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3962070-ef4c-58d1-90b0-e12083f5ee1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319559} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6195742e-d52e-59c7-90f7-2a75e3bfd0be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319565} {:answer/id #uuid "9654c093-21a3-595d-83b2-e94c00af40a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319566} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8cb2d8-ef69-540f-a888-125ad7ad60e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-5{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319567} {:answer/id #uuid "e04d6724-b006-55f2-947c-1ab85a347443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319568} {:answer/id #uuid "65bfd3a7-0113-52c1-9f82-ca0d3afbd56e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6195742e-d52e-59c7-90f7-2a75e3bfd0be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319565} :answer-field/id #uuid "8880541c-2a69-5ab1-ba95-413bd0c95b2f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cba95378-fbb4-59fe-bb7c-3f501d3e7ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335829" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=8,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=4$ and $Q(x)=8.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{4x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y) & ={e}^{4x}\\cdot 8 \\\\ {e}^{4x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4{e}^{4x}\\cdot y & =8{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{4x}) & =8{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{4x})\\text{d}x & =\\int 8{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{4x} & =8(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x})+C \\\\ y{e}^{4x} & =2{e}^{4x}+C \\\\ y & =2+C{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =2+C{e}^{-4(0)} \\\\ -1 & =2+C(1) \\\\ C & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2-3{e}^{-4x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "970ff56e-2e52-52c2-97d3-7f53f0dbb4c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319572} {:answer/id #uuid "707c0390-40f2-5fb6-b7e9-5284a1a1e1dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319573} {:answer/id #uuid "3fcf45f3-bf36-5140-a0f9-a9d66f925998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319574} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fd6a89-a1b4-577f-9dc3-425e3be3ad05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4fd6a89-a1b4-577f-9dc3-425e3be3ad05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319575} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fd74287-9990-5f7e-a03a-4d5cea776822" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "854bcf43-b4dd-5392-81d7-56109d426449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{2}+C{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319577} {:answer/id #uuid "403c74d0-fe50-544e-83ed-a439937b94db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{2}+C{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319578} {:answer/id #uuid "022a3552-46fb-576b-bfac-c07146fdb677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{2}+C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319579} {:answer/id #uuid "f08fe89a-0d9e-5b51-8141-01ddfb4b91f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{2}+C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f08fe89a-0d9e-5b51-8141-01ddfb4b91f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{2}+C{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319580} :answer-field/id #uuid "b54583c2-11ad-5fa1-830e-85ca8fcc9c7e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08d92346-a7e8-58c6-8d60-e4702d743dd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-339138" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-3$ and $Q(x)=x{e}^{3x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{-3x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y) & ={e}^{-3x}\\cdot x{e}^{3x} \\\\ {e}^{-3x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{e}^{-3x}\\cdot y & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-3x}) & =x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{-3x} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}}{2}+C{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7255897-ca61-51f6-b288-7f7fdab7e72a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319583} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2107ca-4041-57f0-ad4d-b4cd01d77c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319584} {:answer/id #uuid "f23f3fdf-48db-5642-b880-93ab3300a0db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319585} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1e7f2d-d753-5bb8-9e4e-4771eb752644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b1e7f2d-d753-5bb8-9e4e-4771eb752644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319586} :answer-field/id #uuid "43dce27c-78a7-53af-a421-0d666e556457" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319582} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7894d8fa-a7a2-58bf-9b7f-522a41fe3240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7x}{6}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319588} {:answer/id #uuid "0604db5e-3279-598b-a8bb-75de180c1f66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7x}{6}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319589} {:answer/id #uuid "5c275c60-3344-5692-b4d8-92e1fbab299c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319590} {:answer/id #uuid "933c36ef-61a4-5af8-b610-00495817af98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7894d8fa-a7a2-58bf-9b7f-522a41fe3240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7x}{6}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319588} :answer-field/id #uuid "57711109-41a9-5bd3-b179-91c5c32d4326" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a456b54-b307-5f53-a533-2575ea7e31b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-339140" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{5}{x}y=7,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below:\n\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{5}{x}$ and $Q(x)=7.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (5/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{5})} \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{5}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{5}{x}y) & ={x}^{5}\\cdot 7 \\\\ {x}^{5}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5{x}^{4}y & =7{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{5})\\,dx & =\\int 7{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{5} & =\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{6}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{7x}{6}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "615f7a80-72d3-5c42-b80a-6b974cb53843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)=x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319594} {:answer/id #uuid "49899034-94cb-5a11-ad00-764fc4d28636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319595} {:answer/id #uuid "dc433d10-5312-563d-becc-a0109d8b6a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319596} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ed86e4-a414-5de3-9213-a95cf9a298ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-x})={e}^{-x}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319597} {:answer/id #uuid "77df38c8-7d9f-5803-ab98-af624a900872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "615f7a80-72d3-5c42-b80a-6b974cb53843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)=x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319594} :answer-field/id #uuid "d768bcb6-12f3-5ae8-820b-17273a5b7c28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3982d0a-beeb-54d4-840e-843a9e35c690" :question/math-academy-id "q-79818" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y={e}^{x},\\,x>0\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)={e}^{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)=x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y) & =x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx) & =x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5372155f-b2c5-5fec-81fe-e0ba5ccff036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot 2)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319601} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3a1c6c-8007-5614-8510-4ad886872f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319602} {:answer/id #uuid "810099ea-8d00-5a5c-99b9-1e4b8220fa01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319603} {:answer/id #uuid "dfef8aa4-b936-5671-9dcc-21b89eeb6a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319604} {:answer/id #uuid "2484f6b4-55e3-58f1-86db-38c1b364636d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2484f6b4-55e3-58f1-86db-38c1b364636d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319605} :answer-field/id #uuid "c62660b7-88d0-59f3-b243-ef8b04326949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e677fdd-ff3d-5255-ae99-601ff749d3b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79819" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\nx\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=1\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
